{"reply":"You can acknowledge the effort without treating it as a reason to give up your voice. For example:

> “I see that you’ve been carrying a lot for the project, and I appreciate the work you’ve put in. I also want to be honest that I felt unsettled when the plan changed after our planning meetings ended. I’d like to understand the reasoning and have a chance to contribute before decisions are finalized. I’m not questioning your commitment—I’m asking for a clearer way for the team to have a say.”

This keeps the focus on what happened, how it affected your participation, and what process you’re asking for—not on whether your lead is a good or bad person. You can appreciate their burden and still ask for shared decision-making.","memory":{"kind":"no_memory_candidate","reason_code":"automatic_capture_disabled"}}
